A||N||3||Seanh||Valve Lash: Is 1 1/2 turns too much?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||03-15-2001||12:42 AM||||I was wondering. I read in the Haynes manual that the initial setting should be 1 1/2 turns after no-play. I was told by GM that it should be 1 turn, and told by some other car guys that it should be 3/4 turns. Which is right??? I know all engines will be a little different, and should be tuned with the valve covers off. What shold I set them to before I run the engine? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||tgowens||03-15-2001||12:58 AM||||here at the shop we set them to 1 1/2 turns.......never had a problem yet.......||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||03-15-2001||01:53 AM||||Unless the engine is being assembled new, go 1/4 to 1/2 turn.  That seems to be the general consensus.  1 & 1/2 seems to be overkill.  When I Fierogod helped me assemble my 2.8 with a new cam, we went 1/2 turn, and it was perfect.  My dad has been building engines for years, and suggested 1/4 to 1/2 turn.||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||03-15-2001||07:03 PM||||I believe you guys are all correct, for all practical purposes.  The important thing is, the plunger should be somewhere in the center of its available travel within the body of the lifter.<P>As long as the plunger can still go down into the lifter far enough to allow for thermal expansion of everything (mostly the valve stem) when the engine warms up, you're okay.<P>I used to sweat this thing a lot, too, before I figured out how the lifters actually work.  What do I use?  One turn, or 3/4 turn - whatever I feel like.  But I'm normally consistent for all the valesv on a given engine I'm working on.        <P>If you don't "prefill" the lifters with oil, after you lash each valve, you can push the "pushrod end" of its rocker down (against the spring in the lifter) by hand, and feel how much room for expansion you left. <P>Use what makes you feel good.  I probably use 1 turn, because it's easy to remember.  But I normally do the "room for expansion" test after each valve to make sure.   ||||reg||6
